INT. MUSEUM - EGYPTIAN WING - NIGHT

Dim. Hushed. Glass cases of scarabs and gold. A security camera pans slowly, red light blinking.

Two figures in dark jumpsuits crouch behind a stone sarcophagus. LUCINDA "LUCKY" ROMANO (30s, braids tucked under a cap, tools in a belt) peers around the lid. Her partner THEODORE "TEDDY BEAR" ADEBAYO (40s, soft-spoken, a bag of cable ties) shifts his weight.

LUCKY
Teddy Bear, camera.

TEDDY BEAR
I see it.

LUCKY
It turns in six seconds.

TEDDY BEAR
I counted five.

LUCKY
Then it turns in five.

The camera sweeps away. Lucky slips out from behind the sarcophagus, Teddy Bear lumbering after her. She kneels at a case. Pulls a thin pick from her belt. He crouches beside her, aims a penlight, steadies the glass with a palm. She slides the pick into the lock. Clicks it. Slides the glass open an inch, lifts the golden scarab on its velvet cushion, drops it into a lined pouch. He holds the lid, lowers it back, wipes the print with a sleeve.

TEDDY BEAR
Done?

LUCKY
Done.

TEDDY BEAR
That was easy.

LUCKY
Don't say that.

TEDDY BEAR
It was.

An alarm shrieks. Red lights spin. A guard's voice crackles somewhere deep in the building.

LUCKY
You said it.

TEDDY BEAR
I'm sorry.

She grabs his sleeve. They bolt for the exit, their footsteps ringing off the marble.
